WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.035
Gloria is 71 and lives alone.

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:05.427
If arthritis or dementia makes it hard to dress, bathe

00:00:05.427 --> 00:00:08.479
or eat without help, who pays for her care?

00:00:08.479 --> 00:00:12.210
Medicare may cover a limited stay for skilled care, but it

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.941
generally does not pay for ongoing help with daily activities alone.

00:00:15.941 --> 00:00:20.011
Medicaid can help people who meet Maryland’s financial and medical eligibility rules.

00:00:20.011 --> 00:00:23.063
Long-term care insurance can pay for help at home,

00:00:23.063 --> 00:00:25.777
in assisted living or in a nursing facility.

00:00:25.777 --> 00:00:29.508
Qualifying policies are an exception to some federal health insurance rules.

00:00:29.508 --> 00:00:32.560
Insurers consider age and health when they price coverage.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.860
Benefits begin when a licensed health professional certifies that the insured meets a trigger.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:45.160
Under the federal tax-qualified standard, the insured must be unable to perform at least

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:51.460
two of six activities of daily living, bathing, dressing, eating, toileting, transferring and continence,

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:57.760
without substantial assistance, for a period expected to last at least 90 days, or

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:04.060
must need substantial supervision because of a severe cognitive impairment such as Alzheimer’s disease.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:09.910
Maryland forbids triggers stricter than these and forbids requiring a prior hospital stay.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:13.510
Gloria’s inability to bathe and dress herself would

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.660
qualify; difficulty with housework alone would not.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.625
Policies describe care by level.

00:01:18.625 --> 00:01:21.770
Skilled care is daily nursing or rehabilitation ordered

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.914
by a physician and delivered by licensed professionals.

00:01:24.914 --> 00:01:29.238
Intermediate care is occasional skilled care, a few times a week.

00:01:29.238 --> 00:01:33.169
Custodial care is help with daily activities by trained aides,

00:01:33.169 --> 00:01:37.100
not nurses, and is what most long-term care claims involve.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.816
Settings vary: a nursing home, an assisted living facility, home health care

00:01:41.816 --> 00:01:46.533
in the insured’s own home, adult day care at a center during

00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:50.857
working hours, and respite care that temporarily relieves a family caregiver.

00:01:50.857 --> 00:01:56.360
Modern comprehensive policies cover all levels in all settings; older facility-only policies did not.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.774
Gloria chooses a daily or monthly benefit amount, say 250

00:02:00.774 --> 00:02:04.746
dollars a day, matched to local nursing home costs.

00:02:04.746 --> 00:02:10.042
She chooses a benefit period, two, three, five years or longer; many

00:02:10.042 --> 00:02:15.338
policies express it as a pool of money, the daily benefit times

00:02:15.338 --> 00:02:20.635
the days, which lasts longer if she uses less than the maximum.

00:02:20.635 --> 00:02:25.490
She chooses an elimination period, often 30, 60 or 90 days

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:29.903
of care before benefits start, which works like a deductible.

00:02:29.903 --> 00:02:34.758
A reimbursement policy pays actual charges up to the daily maximum;

00:02:34.758 --> 00:02:39.613
an indemnity policy pays the full daily benefit regardless of charges.

00:02:39.613 --> 00:02:41.820
Each choice moves the premium.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.583
Four options matter most.

00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:48.872
Inflation protection increases the benefit automatically, typically 5 percent compounded each year,

00:02:48.872 --> 00:02:54.161
so a 250 dollar benefit bought today still buys a day of

00:02:54.161 --> 00:02:58.568
care twenty years from now; Maryland insurers must offer it.

00:02:58.568 --> 00:03:04.297
Nonforfeiture preserves some value if Gloria stops paying, usually a shortened benefit period

00:03:04.297 --> 00:03:09.586
equal to the premiums paid, and Maryland requires it to be offered.

00:03:09.586 --> 00:03:15.757
A guarantee of insurability option lets her buy more coverage later without new underwriting.

00:03:15.757 --> 00:03:19.723
Return of premium refunds premiums less claims at death.

00:03:19.723 --> 00:03:23.690
Exclusions cover pre-existing conditions for up to six months,

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:27.216
war and self-inflicted injury, but never Alzheimer’s disease.

00:03:27.216 --> 00:03:29.860
Underwriting checks health, medications and cognition.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:34.956
A tax-qualified policy meets federal standards: the two-of-six ADL or cognitive

00:03:34.956 --> 00:03:40.051
trigger, guaranteed renewability, required inflation and nonforfeiture offers and consumer protections.

00:03:40.051 --> 00:03:45.610
Its premiums count as deductible medical expenses within age-based limits and its

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:51.169
benefits are tax-free up to a daily cap that is indexed yearly.

00:03:51.169 --> 00:03:57.191
A nonqualified policy may use looser triggers but its tax treatment is uncertain.

00:03:57.191 --> 00:04:02.286
A Partnership policy is a tax-qualified policy approved under Maryland’s Partnership

00:04:02.286 --> 00:04:07.382
program; if Gloria exhausts its benefits and applies for Medicaid, she

00:04:07.382 --> 00:04:12.014
may keep assets equal to the benefits the policy paid.

00:04:12.014 --> 00:04:17.110
Partnership policies must include inflation protection, compound for buyers under 61.
